    <div class="content-text">
      <p><strong>130 - If Greater Than His Fellow His Inclination Is Greater Than Him</strong></p>
      <p><strong>“If someone is greater than his fellow, his inclination is greater than him.”</strong><strong> (Sukkah 52a)</strong></p>
      <p>Through humility he is saved from licentiousness and merits the preservation of the covenant, as our sages taught (Sotah 4b). One who is haughty ultimately stumbles in the matter of a married woman, as it is written: <strong>“And the married woman snares a precious soul.”</strong> (Proverbs 6:26). This connects with the verse: <strong>“Now the man Moses was very humble, more than all people.”</strong> (Numbers 12:3), immediately following the verse: <strong>“And Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses.”</strong> They spoke about the Cushite woman that he had taken—“Cushite” being a term denoting beauty (Sifri; Rashi; see Chizkuni)—and they did not believe true separation was possible. The Torah responded by stating: <strong>“Now the man Moses was very humble…</strong><strong>”</strong>, teaching that through his supreme humility he achieved true separation and had no base union at all. This is likewise explained in Sanhedrin 19b.</p>
      <p>The strength of Joseph lay in the humility of Boaz; the strength of Boaz lay in that of Palti ben Laish—demonstrating that greatness depends on humility. Someone grandeurs himself above his fellow: his inclination is proportionally greater—that inclination dominates him in the urge toward licentiousness. As the Zohar teaches (Vayikra 15b), the essence of the evil inclination is to reveal nakedness.</p>

